# client-billing-settings.identity-seam-default.must-fail.patch
#
# Targets: AC-1 (the SELF arm of the identity seam) — with no
# `.for(client, id)` context, the read falls back to the active session's OWN
# client. The self fallback lives in resolveClientId's `: activeUser.value?.id`
# arm, reached when the scope carries no CLIENT context (ADR-001).
#
# Mutates: client-billing-settings.services.ts resolveClientId — drops the
# `: activeUser.value?.id` fallback so the SELF case resolves `undefined`,
# leaving the session's own read unaddressable (isAddressable gates it off).
#
# Expected RED (integration): client-billing-settings.replay.int.test.ts —
# every scenario boots `{ actor: CLIENT }` with no context id, so the session's
# own read never resolves an id; the shared `open()` Given
# (isAvailable/hasErrors) fails across the file — that breadth is the shared
# seam, not the mutant.
#
# Why the run is slow, not hung: since the one-composable refactor the machine's
# `hasSubscription` guard parks an undefined client id in `subscribing` (waiting
# for the clientId top-up) instead of rejecting NotAuthenticatedError. So under
# this mutant every `isReady()` waits its full 30s bound, and each scenario
# (Background boot + `open()` boot) fails "Test timed out in 60000ms". The whole
# file takes ~17 minutes (17 timeouts). Verify against ONE scenario instead:
#
# Apply / verify / revert:
#   git apply <this file>
#   pnpm exec vitest run src/modules/client-billing-settings/__tests__/client-billing-settings.replay.int.test.ts -t "@AC-1 "   # RED in ~60s
#   git apply -R <this file>
#
--- a/packages/headless/src/modules/client-billing-settings/client-billing-settings.services.ts
+++ b/packages/headless/src/modules/client-billing-settings/client-billing-settings.services.ts
@@ -82,7 +82,7 @@ function resolveClientId(scopeContext?: ScopeContext) {
     scopeContext?.type === ClientBillingSettingsContextTypes.CLIENT &&
     scopeContext.id
       ? scopeContext.id
-      : activeUser.value?.id
+      : undefined
   );
 }
 
